{"id":"swh-t0-0001","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.hello","domain":1,"text_native":"Hamjambo (response: Hatujambo)","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Hello.","source_caption":"Hello","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The default greeting anywhere. Works with every age and every status.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0002","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.morning","domain":1,"text_native":"Asubuhi","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good morning.","source_caption":"morning","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used until roughly midday. Getting the time of day wrong is noticed; not saying it is only noticed by someone expecting it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0003","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.afternoon","domain":1,"text_native":"Mchana","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good afternoon.","source_caption":"afternoon","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The middle of the day. In several languages this is the same word as the evening greeting, and using it late is not an error.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0004","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.evening","domain":1,"text_native":"Jioni","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Good evening.","source_caption":"evening","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"From late afternoon. Often interchangeable with the afternoon greeting.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0005","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.how-are-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Habari yako? (Your news?)","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"How are you?","source_caption":"How are you?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The opening move in almost every conversation. In most of these languages it is a greeting rather than a question and does not expect a literal answer.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0006","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.i-am-well","domain":1,"text_native":"Nzuri, asante.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I am well, thank you.","source_caption":"Fine, thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The expected answer to the greeting above. Answering it wrong reads as evasive in cultures where it is a formula.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0007","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.what-is-your-name","domain":1,"text_native":"Jina lako nani?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"What is your name?","source_caption":"What is your name?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"You get asked this before you get to ask it. Recognising the question is the difference between answering and freezing.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0008","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.where-from","domain":1,"text_native":"Unatoka wapi?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I am from","source_caption":"Where are you from?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The question after your name, and the answer to it. Where you are from changes how you are treated, so it gets asked early.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0009","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.please","domain":1,"text_native":"Tafadhali.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Please.","source_caption":"Please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Not a filler in English, and a grammatical particle in some of these languages — one word that changes a sentence from an order to a request.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0010","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.thank-you","domain":1,"text_native":"Asante (sana).","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Thank you.","source_caption":"Thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The single most useful word in any of these languages. Saying it costs nothing and changes how everything after it is received.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0011","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.youre-welcome","domain":1,"text_native":"Karibu.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"You are welcome.","source_caption":"You're welcome.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"You will be handed it constantly, and there is no way to avoid replying. In a few languages the reply is the same word as 'yes'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0012","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.excuse-me","domain":1,"text_native":"Samahani.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Excuse me.","source_caption":"Excuse me. (getting attention)","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Two different things in one phrase: getting someone's attention, and getting past them. Guessing wrong is jarring — one version walks through a conversation.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0013","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.goodbye","domain":1,"text_native":"Kwaheri.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Goodbye.","source_caption":"Goodbye","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you leave. Leaving without one is noticeably rude in most of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0014","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"Sielewi.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I do not understand.","source_caption":"I do not understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The one phrase that keeps a conversation alive. Without it people stop trying, and with it they often rephrase in simpler words.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"attested","id":"Tatoeba English-Swahili pairs (CC BY 2.0 FR)","url":"https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads","licence":"CC BY 2.0 FR, some CC0","external_id":"4851578","author":"CC"},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0015","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"repair.cannot-hear","domain":12,"text_native":"Utaweza kurudia ulichosema?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I cannot hear you.","source_caption":"Could you say that again?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Distinguishes not hearing from not understanding, because they have different fixes and conflating them produces the wrong one.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"attested","id":"Tatoeba English-Swahili pairs (CC BY 2.0 FR)","url":"https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads","licence":"CC BY 2.0 FR, some CC0","external_id":"1121742","author":"Trailsend"},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0016","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-know","domain":12,"text_native":"Sijui.","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"I do not know.","source_caption":"I do not know.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The honest answer, and in some cultures the only acceptable one — guessing when you don't know reads as deceit rather than as effort.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"attested","id":"Tatoeba English-Swahili pairs (CC BY 2.0 FR)","url":"https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads","licence":"CC BY 2.0 FR, some CC0","external_id":"369342","author":"_undertoad"},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"swh-t0-0017","lang":"swh","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.speak-english","domain":12,"text_native":"Unaongea Kiingereza?","text_romanized":null,"text_english":"Do you speak English?","source_caption":"Do you speak English?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Asked of you constantly, and the question that decides whether anything after it can happen. There is a polite version and a blunt version and the gap between them is wide.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Swahili phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Swahili","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
